\title{Run Multiple Regions and Workers}

\p{
  Add \code{baux.regions(count=4)} to a search pipeline to maintain four local regions. Use \code{baux.complete(state, ids, y)} to return whichever evaluations have finished, and \code{baux.cancel(state, ids)} for work you will not complete. Each call returns the next state. The example below also shows how to save and restore it.
}

\subtree{
  \title{Multi-Region Search & Asynchronous Evaluation}

  \p{
    A region keeps a center, a search length, and a record of recent improvements. Under the TuRBO controller, success and failure counts expand or contract the search length; a sufficiently small region restarts. TuRBO introduced multiple local models with global allocation of evaluations (Eriksson et al. [[EPG+19]](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.01739)). In \strong{baux}, [baux.regions](api-pipelines.html#baux.regions) configures the regional search, while the pipeline chooses the surrogate and acquisition rule.
  }

  \p{
    Candidate generation perturbs coordinates around a region's center. Incumbent sharing determines whether a result from another region may replace that center: \code{origin_only} restricts updates to the originating region, while \code{ellipsoid_ball} also admits better observations inside the region's circumscribing ball. This sharing decision is distinct from the choice of surrogate.
  }

  \p{
    Asynchronous workers return results through [baux.complete](docs.html#baux.complete). Each \code{AskId} identifies a pending evaluation associated with a region and its generation. Restarting a region invalidates its old pending evaluations; foreign, completed, cancelled or invalidated IDs are rejected. [baux.cancel](docs.html#baux.cancel) retires unfinished evaluations without adding observations. Historical results can enter separately through [baux.import_observations](docs.html#baux.import_observations).
  }
}

\subtree{
  \title{Deterministic Rollouts & Checkpointing}

  \p{
    The functional API returns a new [\code{OptimizerState}](docs.html#baux.OptimizerState) after each transition. Explicit PRNG keys let [baux.scan](api-state.html#baux.scan) run a fixed sequence and [baux.pmap](api-state.html#baux.pmap) assign a derived key to each branch (following splittable generators in Claessen & Pałka [[CP13]](https://doi.org/10.1145/2503778.2503784) and Steele et al. [[SLF14]](https://doi.org/10.1145/2660193.2660195)). Use the same key, objective values, and completion order to replay an exact trace.
  }

  \p{
    [baux.freeze](api-state.html#baux.freeze) serializes optimizer state with a BLAKE3 integrity tag, and [baux.thaw](api-state.html#baux.thaw) restores it against a compatible template. The example below verifies that a restored checkpoint matches across four forked rollouts:
  }

  \pre{
import numpy as np
import baux

bounds = np.tile([-3.0, 3.0], (8, 1))
weights = np.geomspace(1.0, 16.0, 8)


def anisotropic_loss(x):
    return -np.sum(weights * (x - 0.35) ** 2 + 0.1 * np.cos(3.0 * x), axis=1)


pipe = (
    baux.lhd(8)
    | baux.enn(k=6)
    | baux.thompson()
    | baux.regions(count=4, sharing="ellipsoid_ball")
)
s0 = baux.init(bounds, pipe, key=baux.key(101))
s_warm, b_init = baux.ask(s0, num_arms=8)
s_warm = baux.complete(s_warm, b_init.ids, anisotropic_loss(b_init.points))

s_live, b1 = baux.ask(s_warm, num_arms=2)
s_live, b2 = baux.ask(s_live, num_arms=2)
s_live = baux.complete(s_live, [b2.ids[0]], anisotropic_loss(b2.points[:1]))
s_live = baux.cancel(s_live, [b2.ids[1]])
s_live = baux.complete(s_live, b1.ids, anisotropic_loss(b1.points))

blob = baux.freeze(s_live)
restored_a = baux.thaw(s0, blob)
restored_b = baux.thaw(s0, baux.freeze(restored_a))
assert baux.freeze(restored_a) == blob
assert baux.digest(s_live) == baux.digest(restored_a) == baux.digest(restored_b)

rollouts_live = baux.pmap(
    s_live,
    baux.key(77),
    branches=4,
    branch_fn=lambda i, st, k: baux.digest(
        baux.scan(st, k, steps=4, eval_fn=anisotropic_loss, num_arms=2)[0]
    ),
)
rollouts_a = baux.pmap(
    restored_a,
    baux.key(77),
    branches=4,
    branch_fn=lambda i, st, k: baux.digest(
        baux.scan(st, k, steps=4, eval_fn=anisotropic_loss, num_arms=2)[0]
    ),
)
rollouts_b = baux.pmap(
    restored_b,
    baux.key(77),
    branches=4,
    branch_fn=lambda i, st, k: baux.digest(
        baux.scan(st, k, steps=4, eval_fn=anisotropic_loss, num_arms=2)[0]
    ),
)
assert rollouts_live == rollouts_a == rollouts_b and len(set(rollouts_live)) == 4
print(f"Verified BLAKE3 digest={baux.digest(s_live)[:16]}... parity across 4 forked rollouts")
  }
}
